investor_module.s:
Create src/lib/investor.ts and wire up the test runner.

The repo has no test framework and must not gain a dependency: Node 22 runs TypeScript test files directly.

STEP 1 — add the test script. Open package.json. The `scripts` block currently ends with:

		"gen": "wrangler types"

Change that line to:

		"gen": "wrangler types",
		"test": "node --test \"src/**/*.test.ts\""

The glob must stay quoted — node's test runner expands it itself, and a bare directory argument does not discover .ts files.

STEP 2 — create src/lib/investor.ts with exactly this content:

export const stage_label: Record<string, string> = {
	i: 'idea',
	b: 'in beta',
	l: 'launched',
	r: 'making revenue',
	s: 'scaling'
};

export const raise_label: Record<string, string> = { y: 'raising now', n: 'not raising' };

export const verify_label: Record<string, string> = {
	v: 'verified by devcircles',
	s: 'self-reported'
};

export type P = Record<string, string>;

const stage_points: Record<string, number> = { s: 20, r: 16, l: 10, b: 5, i: 0 };
const status_points: Record<string, number> = { l: 8, p: 3, u: 0 };

const log_points = (v: string | undefined, mult: number, cap: number): number => {
	const n = Number(v);
	if (!Number.isFinite(n) || n <= 0) return 0;
	return Math.min(cap, Math.round(Math.log10(n) * mult));
};

export const age_days = (hj: string | undefined, now = Date.now()): number | null => {
	const t = Number(hj);
	if (!Number.isFinite(t) || t <= 0) return null;
	return Math.floor((now / 1000 - t) / 86400);
};

export const freshness = (hj: string | undefined, now = Date.now()): number => {
	const d = age_days(hj, now);
	if (d === null) return 0.5;
	if (d <= 90) return 1;
	if (d <= 180) return 0.5;
	return 0;
};

export const is_fresh = (p: P, now = Date.now()): boolean => freshness(p.hj, now) > 0;

export const as_of = (hj: string | undefined): string => {
	const t = Number(hj);
	if (!Number.isFinite(t) || t <= 0) return 'date not given';
	return (
		'as of ' +
		new Date(t * 1000)
			.toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' })
			.toLowerCase()
	);
};

export const momentum = (p: P, now = Date.now()): number =>
	Math.round(
		freshness(p.hj, now) *
			((p.hm ? 10 : 0) +
				(p.hv === 'v' ? 5 : 0) +
				(p.m === 'y' ? 20 : 0) +
				log_points(p.a, 4, 20) +
				log_points(p.q, 5, 20) +
				(stage_points[p.sg] ?? 0) +
				(p.ra === 'y' ? 6 : 0))
	) + (status_points[p.r] ?? 0);

export const by_momentum = (a: P, b: P, now = Date.now()): number =>
	momentum(b, now) - momentum(a, now) || (a.n ?? '').localeCompare(b.n ?? '');

export const headline = (p: P): { v: string; l: string; hv: string; hj: string } | null => {
	const hj = p.hj ?? '';
	if (p.hm) return { v: p.hm, l: p.hl || 'headline metric', hv: p.hv || 's', hj };
	if (p.k) return { v: p.k, l: 'proudest metric', hv: 's', hj };
	if (p.q) return { v: p.q, l: 'users', hv: 's', hj };
	if (p.m === 'y' && p.a) return { v: p.a, l: 'monthly revenue (₦)', hv: 's', hj };
	return null;
};

Why a log scale: a company with 10,000 users is not a thousand times better than one with 10, and a raw sum would let a single inflated number own the whole page forever. Logs compress the difference to something an investor's eye reads as a ranking rather than a scoreboard. The caps stop one self-reported figure dominating.

Why freshness multiplies everything except status: `r` is checked mechanically by scripts/probe.mjs, so it stays true without anyone doing anything. Every other signal here is a human's word about a moment that has passed. Half weight at three months and none at six is not a punishment, it is an accurate statement of how much a six-month-old startup number is worth. An undated figure gets 0.5 rather than 1, because a number with no date attached has already failed the only test that matters.

STEP 3 — place the test, do not type it, do not open it to check it, do not adapt an import that looks wrong:

cp plan/tests/src/lib/investor.test.ts.txt src/lib/investor.test.ts

The import inside it ends in '.ts' on purpose — node's TypeScript stripping requires the real file extension.

STEP 4 — run `pnpm test` and `pnpm check`. Both must be clean. If a test fails, the implementation above was mistyped; fix src/lib/investor.ts, never the test.

probe_script.s:
Create scripts/probe.mjs — a mechanical link checker that gathers only facts a machine can observe, so no judgement and no invented numbers can enter the data.

It reads every product from Qdrant, fetches each product url with a real browser user agent (several of these sites, campuspadi.com among them, return HTTP 403 to bare fetchers and render normally with a browser UA), and writes plan/data/probe.json.

Create scripts/probe.mjs with exactly this content:

import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';

const env = {};
for (const l of readFileSync(new URL('../.env', import.meta.url), 'utf8').split('\n'))
	if (l.includes('=')) env[l.slice(0, l.indexOf('=')).trim()] = l.slice(l.indexOf('=') + 1).trim();

const UA =
	'Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/131.0 Safari/537.36';

const res = await fetch(env.QDRANT_URL + '/collections/i/points/scroll', {
	method: 'POST',
	headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json', 'api-key': env.QDRANT_KEY },
	body: JSON.stringify({
		filter: { must: [{ key: 's', match: { value: 'adca' } }, { key: 't', match: { value: 'p' } }] },
		limit: 500,
		with_payload: true,
		with_vector: false
	})
});
const products = (await res.json()).result.points.map((p) => p.payload);

const probe = async (p) => {
	const out = { g: p.g, u: p.u, ok: false, status: 0, title: '', pricing: false, signup: false, app: '' };
	if (!p.u) return out;
	try {
		const r = await fetch(p.u, { headers: { 'user-agent': UA }, redirect: 'follow', signal: AbortSignal.timeout(25000) });
		out.status = r.status;
		out.ok = r.ok;
		const html = await r.text();
		out.title = (html.match(/<title[^>]*>([^<]{0,160})/i)?.[1] ?? '').trim();
		const text = html.toLowerCase();
		out.pricing = /pricing|\bplans?\b|per month|\/mo\b|₦|subscribe/.test(text);
		out.signup = /sign ?up|get started|create (an )?account|register|join/.test(text);
		out.app = html.match(/https?:\/\/(play\.google\.com|apps\.apple\.com)\/[^"'\s<]+/i)?.[0] ?? '';
	} catch (err) {
		out.error = String(err.message ?? err);
	}
	return out;
};

const results = [];
for (const p of products) {
	const r = await probe(p);
	results.push(r);
	console.log(r.g, r.status || r.error || '', r.ok ? 'ok' : 'FAIL');
}

writeFileSync(
	new URL('../plan/data/probe.json', import.meta.url),
	JSON.stringify({ checked: new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10), results }, null, 1)
);
console.log('probed', results.length);

Run it with `node scripts/probe.mjs`. It is sequential on purpose — 43 parallel fetches from one IP gets rate limited and produces false failures. Expect it to take two or three minutes and for a handful of sites to fail; that is data, not an error.

probe_apply.s:
Create scripts/probe_apply.mjs, which turns plan/data/probe.json into the two payload fields a machine is allowed to decide: `vd` (the date the link was last verified) and `ev` (what was observed).

It must not touch `r`, `sg`, `hm` or any other field — status and stage are editorial and builder-owned, and a script guessing at them is exactly the fabrication this plan exists to avoid. Where the probe disagrees with the stored status it prints a review line for a human, and changes nothing.

Create scripts/probe_apply.mjs with exactly this content:

import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { createHash } from 'node:crypto';

const env = {};
for (const l of readFileSync(new URL('../.env', import.meta.url), 'utf8').split('\n'))
	if (l.includes('=')) env[l.slice(0, l.indexOf('=')).trim()] = l.slice(l.indexOf('=') + 1).trim();

const uuid_from = (s) => {
	const h = createHash('sha256').update('adca:' + s).digest('hex');
	return `${h.slice(0, 8)}-${h.slice(8, 12)}-4${h.slice(13, 16)}-8${h.slice(17, 20)}-${h.slice(20, 32)}`;
};

const { checked, results } = JSON.parse(
	readFileSync(new URL('../plan/data/probe.json', import.meta.url), 'utf8')
);

for (const r of results) {
	const bits = [];
	if (r.ok) bits.push('link reachable');
	else bits.push('link did not respond');
	if (r.pricing) bits.push('public pricing');
	if (r.signup) bits.push('open signup');
	if (r.app) bits.push('mobile app listing');
	const payload = { vd: checked, ev: bits.join(' · ') };
	const res = await fetch(env.QDRANT_URL + '/collections/i/points/payload?wait=true', {
		method: 'POST',
		headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json', 'api-key': env.QDRANT_KEY },
		body: JSON.stringify({ payload, points: [uuid_from(r.g)] })
	});
	console.log(r.g, payload.ev, res.status);
}

console.log('\nreview these by hand — probe disagrees with stored status:');
for (const r of results) if (!r.ok) console.log(' ', r.g, r.status || r.error);

Note it uses POST /points/payload, which merges rather than replaces, so nothing else on the point is touched.

Run `node scripts/probe_apply.mjs`. Every line must end in 200.

headline_seed.s:
Apply the planner-authored headline metrics.

plan/data/headline.json holds every figure that has a defensible source today, and only those. It is deliberately short. Most of the 43 have no public number, and the correct behaviour is to leave them empty so the card says so — an empty slot is what makes builders fill the form in, and a fabricated one would destroy the credibility this whole surface is built on.

Create scripts/headline_apply.mjs with exactly this content:

import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { createHash } from 'node:crypto';

const env = {};
for (const l of readFileSync(new URL('../.env', import.meta.url), 'utf8').split('\n'))
	if (l.includes('=')) env[l.slice(0, l.indexOf('=')).trim()] = l.slice(l.indexOf('=') + 1).trim();

const uuid_from = (s) => {
	const h = createHash('sha256').update('adca:' + s).digest('hex');
	return `${h.slice(0, 8)}-${h.slice(8, 12)}-4${h.slice(13, 16)}-8${h.slice(17, 20)}-${h.slice(20, 32)}`;
};

const rows = JSON.parse(readFileSync(new URL('../plan/data/headline.json', import.meta.url), 'utf8'));

for (const row of rows) {
	const { g, ...payload } = row;
	const res = await fetch(env.QDRANT_URL + '/collections/i/points/payload?wait=true', {
		method: 'POST',
		headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json', 'api-key': env.QDRANT_KEY },
		body: JSON.stringify({ payload, points: [uuid_from(g)] })
	});
	console.log(g, JSON.stringify(payload), res.status);
}

Run `node scripts/headline_apply.mjs`. Every line must end in 200. Do not add rows to plan/data/headline.json — if you believe a product deserves a number that is not in the file, `--block` this step and say which product and what source you found.

edit_action_persists.s:
The edit form now posts five new fields that the server action drops on the floor.

FILE: src/routes/[slug]/edit/+page.server.ts

Current line inside the `save` action:

		const metrics = { d: v('d'), q: v('q'), m: v('m'), a: v('a'), z: v('z'), k: v('k') };

Replace it with:

		const metrics = { d: v('d'), q: v('q'), m: v('m'), a: v('a'), z: v('z'), k: v('k') };
		const investor = {
			hm: v('hm'),
			hl: v('hl'),
			sg: v('sg'),
			ra: v('ra'),
			rt: v('ra') === 'y' ? v('rt') : '',
			fp: v('fp'),
			hj: String(Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000))
		};

Then find the payload object further down:

			b,
			...metrics
		};

and change it to:

			b,
			...metrics,
			...investor
		};

`hj` is the freshness stamp and it is computed here on the server, never read from the form — a builder must not be able to claim their year-old number was confirmed today. Saving the form IS the confirmation: a builder who opens their page, looks at the figures and presses save is asserting they are current, which is exactly what the stamp records.

`hv` is deliberately absent: verification is the devcircles team's word, never the builder's, so a builder editing their own page can never mark their own number verified. `ev` and `vd` are equally absent — they are written only by scripts/probe_apply.mjs.

The spread order matters. `...ep` at the top of the payload carries the existing point forward, and `...investor` last means the form always wins for the fields it owns.

confirm_action.s:
Give a builder a one-click way to say 'still accurate'.

Re-entering figures is the expensive part, and expensive means never. Confirming is cheap, and a confirmed old number is worth far more to an investor than a fresh-looking undated one. This single button is the difference between figures that get refreshed and figures that quietly rot.

FILE: src/routes/[slug]/edit/+page.server.ts

Add a third action alongside `save` and `del`. Insert it immediately before the `del:` action:

	confirm: async ({ params, locals }) => {
		const { id, ep } = await load_owned(params, locals);
		await upsert(env, [
			{ id, payload: { ...ep, hj: String(Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000)) } }
		]);
		throw redirect(303, '/' + params.slug + '?confirmed=1');
	},

It changes exactly one field. `load_owned` already throws 403 for anyone who is not the owner, so no extra check is needed.

FILE: src/routes/[slug]/+page.svelte

In the script block add to the existing '$lib/investor' import: `age_days`. Then add:

	const stamp_age = $derived(age_days(p.hj));

Insert, immediately after the `edit your page` link's containing div and before the `<h1`, this owner-only prompt:

	{#if is_owner && (stamp_age === null || stamp_age > 60)}
		<div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 rounded-lg bg-ochre/10 p-4 text-sm">
			<span class="text-ink/75">
				{stamp_age === null
					? 'your figures have never been dated, so investors see them as unconfirmed.'
					: `your figures were last confirmed ${stamp_age} days ago and are sinking down the homepage.`}
			</span>
			<form method="POST" action="/{p.g}/edit?/confirm" use:enhance>
				<button type="submit" class="rounded-full bg-cobalt px-4 py-2 text-xs font-medium text-white hover:bg-cobalt/90">
					still accurate
				</button>
			</form>
			<a href="/{p.g}/edit" class="text-cobalt hover:underline">or update them</a>
		</div>
	{/if}

That needs `import { enhance } from '$app/forms';` in the same script block.

Also handle the redirect flag. The file already renders banners for `submitted` and `saved` search params; add a third branch to that same `{#if}` chain, matching the existing markup exactly:

	{:else if page.url.searchParams.has('confirmed')}
		<div class="mt-6 rounded-lg bg-teal-brand/10 p-4 text-sm text-ink">confirmed — dated today.</div>

The prompt is shown to the owner only. An investor never sees a nag; they see the date and draw their own conclusion, which is the entire point.

home_filters.s:
Turn the filter row into the one an investor actually uses. Today it is a search box and a `live only` toggle. Sector is not how anyone screens deals; stage and money are.

FILE: src/routes/+page.svelte

In the script block replace:

	let live_only = $state(false);

with:

	let live_only = $state(false);
	let earning_only = $state(false);
	let raising_only = $state(false);

and replace the `filtered` derivation:

	const filtered = $derived(
		data.p.filter(
			(x) =>
				(!live_only || x.r === 'l') &&
				(x.n + ' ' + x.o).toLowerCase().includes(qy.toLowerCase())
		)
	);

with:

	const filtered = $derived(
		data.p.filter(
			(x) =>
				(!live_only || x.r === 'l') &&
				(!earning_only || x.m === 'y') &&
				(!raising_only || x.ra === 'y') &&
				(x.n + ' ' + x.o).toLowerCase().includes(qy.toLowerCase())
		)
	);

and extend `is_filtering`:

	const is_filtering = $derived(qy !== '' || live_only || earning_only || raising_only);

In the markup, the existing `live only` button is a `<button type="button" onclick={() => (live_only = !live_only)}` carrying a long conditional class. Duplicate it twice, immediately after, changing only the state variable and the label text: one for `earning_only` labelled `making revenue`, one for `raising_only` labelled `raising now`. Keep the classes character for character so the three read as one control group.

Order them: `raising now`, `making revenue`, `live only`. That order is not cosmetic — the first chip is the one most people press, and 'raising now' is the only filter that answers 'is there anything here for me to do today'.

deal_card.s:
Create the card that does the actual work — the thing an investor's eye lands on.

Create src/lib/deal_card.svelte. It renders one product as a row with, in reading order: the founder's face, the name and status, the headline number set large, the one-liner, and the stage and raising badges. Every element is skipped cleanly when its data is missing.

Use exactly this content:

<script lang="ts">
	import { headline, stage_label, raise_label, verify_label, is_fresh, as_of } from '$lib/investor';
	import { fmt_num } from '$lib/fmt';
	import StatusPill from '$lib/status_pill.svelte';

	let { p }: { p: Record<string, string> & { b?: Record<string, string> } } = $props();

	const hd = $derived(headline(p));
	const current = $derived(is_fresh(p));
	const who = $derived(p.b?.n ?? '');
	const initials = $derived(
		who
			.split(' ')
			.filter(Boolean)
			.slice(0, 2)
			.map((s) => s[0])
			.join('')
			.toLowerCase()
	);
</script>

<a
	href="/{p.g}"
	class="grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] items-start gap-4 py-5 transition-colors hover:bg-ink/[0.03] sm:grid-cols-[auto_1fr_auto] sm:items-center sm:gap-6"
>
	{#if p.fp}
		<img src={p.fp} alt="" loading="lazy" class="h-12 w-12 shrink-0 rounded-full object-cover" />
	{:else}
		<span
			class="font-display flex h-12 w-12 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-cobalt/10 text-sm font-medium text-cobalt"
		>
			{initials || '—'}
		</span>
	{/if}

	<div class="min-w-0">
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
			<span class="font-display font-medium text-ink">{p.n}</span>
			<StatusPill r={p.r} />
			{#if p.ra === 'y' && current}
				<span class="rounded-full bg-coral px-2.5 py-1 text-[10px] font-medium tracking-wide text-white uppercase">
					{raise_label.y}
				</span>
			{/if}
		</div>
		<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-ink/70">{p.o}</p>
		<div class="mt-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 text-xs text-ink/50">
			{#if p.sg && stage_label[p.sg]}<span>{stage_label[p.sg]}</span>{/if}
			{#if who}<span>{who.toLowerCase()}</span>{/if}
		</div>
	</div>

	<div class="col-span-2 sm:col-span-1 sm:w-44 sm:text-right">
		{#if hd}
			<div class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold {current ? 'text-ink' : 'text-ink/40'}">
				{fmt_num(hd.v)}
			</div>
			<div class="text-xs text-ink/60">{current ? hd.l : 'last reported ' + hd.l}</div>
			<div class="text-[10px] tracking-wide text-ink/40 uppercase">
				{verify_label[hd.hv]} · {as_of(hd.hj)}
			</div>
		{:else}
			<div class="text-xs text-ink/40">no numbers shared yet</div>
		{/if}
	</div>
</a>

The `no numbers shared yet` state is load-bearing and must not be prettified away or hidden: it is the only pressure on a builder to fill the form in, and an investor who sees forty cards with numbers and three without has learned something true.

The date line is equally load-bearing and must never be dropped to tidy the card. A figure without its date is the actual failure mode of every directory like this: it reads as current forever and is wrong within months. Once a figure has fully decayed the card keeps showing it, dimmed and in the past tense as `last reported`, and the raising badge disappears, because a round announced eight months ago has almost certainly closed and telling an investor otherwise wastes the one thing they cannot get back.

`fmt_num` passes non-numeric strings through untouched, so a headline of `first paying customer` renders as itself while `2400` renders as `2,400`.

home_two_views.s:
Make the traction-ordered list the default view and demote sector browsing to a toggle.

An investor arriving cold does not think 'show me fintech', they think 'show me what is working'. Sector-first structure buries the strongest company on the page under a heading it has nothing to do with. But sector browsing is genuinely useful once someone has a thesis, so it stays — one tap away.

FILE: src/routes/+page.svelte

In the script block add the import:

	import { by_momentum } from '$lib/investor';
	import DealCard from '$lib/deal_card.svelte';

and the state plus the ordered list:

	let by_sector = $state(false);
	const ranked = $derived([...filtered].sort(by_momentum));

In the markup, wrap the existing `{#each groups as g (g.c)}` block so it only renders in sector mode, and put the ranked list in front of it. Replace the opening of the products section, currently:

	<section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-16">
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-14">
			{#each groups as g (g.c)}

with:

	<section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-16">
		<div class="mb-8 flex items-center gap-4 text-sm">
			<button
				type="button"
				onclick={() => (by_sector = false)}
				class={by_sector ? 'text-ink/50 hover:text-ink' : 'font-medium text-cobalt underline'}
			>
				by traction
			</button>
			<button
				type="button"
				onclick={() => (by_sector = true)}
				class={by_sector ? 'font-medium text-cobalt underline' : 'text-ink/50 hover:text-ink'}
			>
				by sector
			</button>
		</div>
		{#if !by_sector}
			<div class="flex flex-col divide-y divide-ink/10 border-y border-ink/10">
				{#each ranked as it (it.g)}
					<DealCard p={it} />
				{/each}
			</div>
		{/if}
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-14" class:hidden={!by_sector}>
			{#each groups as g (g.c)}

The rest of the sector block, down to and including its closing `{/each}`, is untouched. The `no products match your search` paragraph must end up outside the `class:hidden` div so it still shows in traction mode — move it to just before the section's closing `</section>` tag and leave its markup otherwise identical.

Also swap the inner rows of the sector view to the same component so the two views cannot drift: inside `{#each g.items as it (it.g)}`, replace the whole `<a href="/{it.g}" ...>...</a>` element with `<DealCard p={it} />`.

product_headline.s:
Put the number, the stage and the raise above the fold on the product page, directly under the one-liner, so a reader who arrived from a shared link learns the case in one screen.

FILE: src/routes/[slug]/+page.svelte

In the script block add:

	import { headline, stage_label, raise_label, verify_label, is_fresh, as_of } from '$lib/investor';

	const hd = $derived(headline(p));
	const current = $derived(is_fresh(p));

The one-liner paragraph currently reads:

	<p class="mt-8 max-w-3xl text-2xl leading-snug font-medium text-ink">{p.o}</p>

Insert immediately after it:

	{#if hd || p.sg || p.ra === 'y'}
		<div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap items-end gap-x-12 gap-y-6">
			{#if hd}
				<div>
					<div class="font-display text-5xl font-semibold {current ? 'text-ink' : 'text-ink/40'}">
						{fmt_num(hd.v)}
					</div>
					<div class="mt-1 text-sm text-ink/60">{current ? hd.l : 'last reported ' + hd.l}</div>
					<div class="text-xs tracking-wide text-ink/40 uppercase">
						{verify_label[hd.hv]} · {as_of(hd.hj)}
					</div>
				</div>
			{/if}
			{#if p.sg && stage_label[p.sg]}
				<div>
					<div class="font-display text-xl font-medium text-ink">{stage_label[p.sg]}</div>
					<div class="mt-1 text-sm text-ink/60">stage</div>
				</div>
			{/if}
			{#if p.ra === 'y' && current}
				<div>
					<div class="font-display text-xl font-medium text-coral">{p.rt || raise_label.y}</div>
					<div class="mt-1 text-sm text-ink/60">raising</div>
				</div>
			{/if}
		</div>
	{/if}

And change the `{#if hd || p.sg || p.ra === 'y'}` opening condition to `{#if hd || p.sg || (p.ra === 'y' && current)}` so a page whose only investor signal is a decayed raise does not render an empty band.

